title: POST /repos/:repo/metarecords/trash
tags: [[HTTP endpoint]] Trash
kind: reference
audience: dev
summary: The trash-bin's only daemon endpoint: delete the metarecords a trashing takes, in one revision stamped trash.

```
POST /repos/:repo/metarecords/trash
{"uuids": ["<hex>", ...], "force": false}
→ {"deleted": N}
```

Deletes the given metarecords in a single revision stamped `origin = 'trash'`,
each leaving its duplicate group first ([[Duplicate groups]]). The client calls it after capturing the
metarecords and before moving the bytes ([[What trashing does]]).

* `409` — a metarecord outside the set references one inside it; the message names up to ten referrers, and nothing is written. `force: true` deletes anyway.
* `400` — the set holds the repository root, `force` or not.

No cap on the number of uuids: the 2 MiB body limit already bounds a call at tens
of thousands of them, and a trashing must be one revision — paging it would break
the thing it is for.

The reference check is served from the index's reverse maps, one bitmap lookup per
reference-typed field name: proportional to the number of `ref`/`tree_ref`
fields, never a scan of the values. `external_ref` and `ref_base` values are not
in those maps and so not detected.
